The two backends disagree about SysV's aggregate classification on purpose, and
this fixture is what says so.

The IR backend implements the rule: an aggregate of at most sixteen bytes is
split into eightbytes, each classified INTEGER or SSE, and passed and returned
in one register apiece - rdi:rsi, xmm0:xmm1, rax:rdx, and the mixed orders. That
is what the ABI says and what gcc and tcc do, and it is checked against gcc by
codegen/bugs/eightbyte_aggregates.c. The legacy backend passes every composite
wider than a word on the stack and returns it through a hidden pointer, down an
address-based path that never reaches a register class at all, and is not going
to be taught otherwise.

So every shape here but struct I8 (one eightbyte) and struct Big (too large for
either) now travels differently depending on which backend compiled the caller.
Each backend is right linked against itself; the disagreement is only visible
across a link, which is what this suite is. See crossabi/sse_aggregate.c for the
single-eightbyte half of the same divergence.

What this costs: the hidden-return-pointer and large-struct checks below no
longer report across backends, because the small shapes fail first. Nothing else
covers those two across a link.

Delete this file if the legacy side ever learns the rule - the runner flags a
muted test that starts passing.
